entityDescription:
UNEP/GRID-Warsaw Centre was established on 17 September 1991 pursuant to the official agreement signed between the Director of the United Nations Environment Programme (UNEP) and the Polish Government (Ministry of the Environment, Forestry, and Natural Resources), as a centre working within the Global Resource Information Database (GRID) network. Formally, the Centre has been established in the structures of the National Foundation for Environmental Protection (hence, it has the non-profit status).
The Centre actively co-operates with self-government authorities, educational institutions and private companies pursuing corporate social responsibility (CSR) principles in a wide range of projects devoted to effective environmental management, application of remote sensing, geographic information systems (GIS), and environmental education.
Specific areas of expertise and activity of the UNEP/GRID-Warsaw Centre entail the following:
&middot; geoinformation and remote sensing: analyses of spatial data on environmental and socio-economic phenomena and processes; geodatabase development and maintenance (incl. data harmonisation according to the INSPIRE implementing rules); development and market application of GIS solutions and geoportals; practical implementation of result of scientific discovery related to novel geoinformation tools and methods;
&middot; environmental governance and reporting: thematic mapping and geovisualisation, incl. 3D modelling; development of GIS and ICT applications to support decision making and spatial planning by public administration units; use of mobile GIS-based tools and techniques to support monitoring and rapid mapping activities; implementation of the INSPIRE Directive in Poland;
&middot; capacity building: specialized trainings, workshops, seminars, conferences; e-Learning courses; educational materials and publications &ndash; targeted primarily at public administration bodies and related to the use of remote sensing tools, GIS tools and applications (with focus on open source solutions), spatial data for environmental management, spatial planning, crisis management, etc.
&middot; environmental campaigns, environmental education, public participation: using modern multimedia and other ICT solutions; youth education and awareness building on issues related to environment/nature protection, community involvement, building ICT literacy; fostering public and inter-institutional consultations, e.g. in spatial planning procedures, aided by geoinformation tools and methods (geo-consultations); citizen science; CSR and sustainable business practices;
